 "genre_note": "Twenty news articles published by Fortune and the Sydney Morning Herald (much of it syndicated AP and Bloomberg copy). The \"business\" label is loose: only about half is finance/markets/corporate. The rest is national politics (the House speaker fight), international affairs and conflict economics (Gaza, Egypt), science (the chemistry Nobel), sports (Billie Jean King), entertainment/exhibition economics (the Eras Tour film), a generational trend piece (Gen Z), a long investigative feature (the Epstein estate in the US Virgin Islands), and two Fortune Recommends credit-card product reviews that are structurally not news at all.\n\nDecomposition followed the documents' own shape. Single-story articles decomposed one-to-one into a sub-plan. Two pairs shared entities and got shared notes rather than near-duplicates: doc_0223 + doc_0377 are the same Jim Jordan speaker race two days apart, so `house_gop_narrow_majority_vote_math`, `jim_jordan_political_profile_and_record`, `trump_backing_of_jim_jordan_speaker_bid`, `republican_holdouts_case_against_jim_jordan`, `patrick_mchenry_expanded_speaker_pro_tempore_proposal`, `house_speakership_vacancy_paralysis_after_mccarthy_ouster` and `hakeem_jeffries_bipartisan_offer_to_reopen_the_house` each draw blocks from both. doc_0112 + doc_0479 are two takes on the same 2023 housing thesis, so `housing_market_1980s_analogy_rather_than_2008`, `volcker_and_powell_rate_hiking_cycles_compared` and `millennials_as_the_new_boomer_homebuying_cohort` are shared across both (Jeseo Park of BofA appears in both documents by name).\n\nBuilding-block distribution is exactly what the guidance predicts. Of 170 notes: empirical_observation and concept dominate; argument and counter_argument are unusually well represented because this corpus is heavy with attributed claims and their rebuttals \u2014 prosecution vs. defense (doc_0031), the sisters' petition vs. the Rogers spokesperson (doc_0156), Blanchard/Constancio vs. Nagel and Lagarde vs. Powell (doc_0503), Figure AI's purist humanoid thesis vs. Boston Dynamics' non-humanoid result (doc_0421), George vs. Governor Bryan (doc_0477). Four procedure notes exist because four documents genuinely contain ordered steps: the Nobel nomination/selection process, carriers' capacity-management levers, and the two card redemption workflows. Only four hypothesis notes were written, and each is an explicit forward-looking prediction from a named source (Goldman's price path, Egypt-offers-Saudi-co-leadership, the 2023 box office forecast, Shelton's 10-to-20-year humanoid ubiquity claim). No navigation notes were produced.\n\nCoverage: seventeen of twenty documents land above 92% of words assigned; every dropped block in those is an article title, a section header, a \"Read More\" teaser, a contributor byline, or a republication/attribution notice. The two credit-card reviews are the exceptions, at roughly 90% (doc_0470) and 87% (doc_0471) \u2014 their dropped material is almost entirely one-to-twelve-word section headers, FAQ question stubs, and boilerplate legal/rate disclaimers, which is the genre's chrome. Judgement calls made in favour of assignment: doc_0412's interview question blocks 14, 16 and 18 were kept because they carry substantive framing and the PWHL city list, while the two purely contentless prompts (20, 22) were dropped; doc_0332 block 10, the AP/OpenAI licensing disclosure, was assigned to the partnerships note rather than dropped as boilerplate; doc_0223 block 19 (\"A betrayal,\" Banks) reads like a contentless reaction but records the hard-right rejection of the McHenry off-ramp, so it stayed with block 18. The one correction notice in the corpus, doc_0112 block 20, was inspected and dropped: it says only that a \"mistaken term used to refer to Jeseo Park\" was fixed, without naming the term or touching any claim in the article, so it carries no scope condition."
